1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,440
How does one corner of a game building end up
at a specific screen position?

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:07,100
First we follow landmarks in real games and
calculate one

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:11,300
point from object coordinates to camera
coordinates.

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,840
Next we clip the viewing volume, divide,

5
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:16,560
and map through an output rectangle to obtain
the screen coordinates.

6
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:19,860
Finally, include the origin and size of a
smaller viewport and

7
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:23,740
complete the calculation of where that same
point appears on screen.

8
00:00:24,210 --> 00:00:30,050
This is official Portal2 gameplay. Follow both
the moving viewpoint and the central pillar.

9
00:00:30,310 --> 00:00:32,590
The pillar changes screen position without
moving,

10
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:34,630
while a device behind it becomes hidden.

11
00:00:34,810 --> 00:00:37,390
To calculate that difference,

12
00:00:37,390 --> 00:00:39,470
express the same point in world coordinates
and camera coordinates.

13
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:42,980
Matching matrix names do not guarantee
matching formulas.

14
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:47,240
Today we use left-handed view coordinates with
forward plusz.

15
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:51,380
Vectors are rows with matrices multiplied on
the right;

16
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:54,420
distinguish this from the earlier rotation
lesson’s column convention.

17
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:56,840
Clip depth runs from0 tow;

18
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:01,300
after division depth runs from0 to1. Keep the
stages distinct.

19
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:07,940
The screen origin is top-left andy increases
downward, opposite the camera’s upwardy.

20
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:12,140
Record space,vector convention,depth range and
screen origin separately;

21
00:01:12,460 --> 00:01:16,000
an API name alone cannot settle them all.

22
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:19,560
Recall the earlier matrix derivations as
needed;

23
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:23,220
this lesson connects every intermediate value
of one point to its final result.

24
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:28,470
Choose a corner of a small box at local

25
00:01:28,470 --> 00:01:32,390
coordinates(1,0,0); its position hasw1.

26
00:01:32,850 --> 00:01:40,130
Translate the box by(3,2,5), with no rotation
or scale in this first example.

27
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:43,770
Adding the translation gives world
coordinates(4,2,5);

28
00:01:44,050 --> 00:01:48,510
the local coordinates remain valid in their
own frame.

29
00:01:48,750 --> 00:01:51,870
For our row vectors, translation occupies the
last row,

30
00:01:51,870 --> 00:01:55,070
rather than the last column of a column-vector
matrix.

31
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:59,290
A direction hasw0, so transforming
direction(1,0,0)

32
00:01:59,290 --> 00:02:04,570
by the same translation does not add(3,2,5).

33
00:02:04,890 --> 00:02:10,710
Normals are directions, but nonuniform scaling
requires a

34
00:02:11,170 --> 00:02:13,670
separate normal transformation, covered in the
lighting lesson.

35
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:18,660
Choose a front roof corner in Townscaper.

36
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:22,780
New blocks grow from a platform and change the
structure.

37
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:28,580
Distinguish newly created corners from the
original landmark;

38
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:32,140
comparing different points cannot verify one
coordinate transformation.

39
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:36,440
Orbiting reveals the long roof’s sides and
interior opening,

40
00:02:36,700 --> 00:02:39,820
while the original corner keeps changing
screen position.

41
00:02:39,869 --> 00:02:43,360
Connect these visible changes to separate
world

42
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:45,920
placement and camera-relative coordinates.

43
00:02:46,102 --> 00:02:50,100
To record a world position, identify a point
in the object’s frame and

44
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:52,880
store the resulting world-space position after
its model transform.

45
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:57,720
Orbiting a camera does not require rewriting
every building’s world coordinates;

46
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:00,380
a later step changes the viewing frame.

47
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:04,120
These pixels do not reveal the game’s
matrices.

48
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:10,220
Our local point and translation are separately
defined teaching values.

49
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:14,700
Separating construction changes from viewpoint
changes is

50
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:17,240
the first habit for inspecting intermediate
coordinates.

51
00:03:17,770 --> 00:03:25,230
Place the camera at(2,1,1), aligned with the
world’s right,up and forward axes.

52
00:03:25,610 --> 00:03:30,190
Subtracting the camera position from world
point(4,2,5)

53
00:03:30,190 --> 00:03:35,150
gives view point(2,1,4): right2,up1,forward4.

54
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:38,930
A rotated camera also requires expressing the

55
00:03:39,270 --> 00:03:44,010
displacement along its right,up and forward
axes.

56
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:48,250
The view matrix is the inverse camera-to-world
transform;

57
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:52,010
check that it maps the camera’s own position
to the origin.

58
00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:55,630
An orthonormal rotation can be inverted by
transposing;

59
00:03:56,030 --> 00:04:00,190
this does not extend blindly to arbitrary
scale or shear.

60
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:05,430
For our rows, multiply the point by model,view
and projection in that order;

61
00:04:05,810 --> 00:04:08,610
column-vector notation reverses the written
product.

62
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:12,240
Our view point is(2,1,4). The simplest
perspective

63
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:18,000
preparation copies itsz4 into the fourth
componentw.

64
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:20,380
No division by4 has occurred yet;

65
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:23,820
this step only prepares a four-component clip
representation.

66
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:29,180
Dividing this simplified result
gives(0.5,0.25,1),

67
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:33,680
but this is not a complete near-to-far depth
mapping.

68
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:37,000
The complete matrix also prepares both zoom
axes

69
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,460
and near-to-far depth; we fill those terms
next.

70
00:04:41,980 --> 00:04:43,980
Orthographic projection keepsw1,

71
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:49,220
so the same division stage does not introduce
distance-based shrinking.

72
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:54,280
The common representation also supports
asymmetric views and tiled rendering;

73
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:57,620
left and right bounds need not be symmetric.

74
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:03,820
Homogeneous division connects different
projections through one convention;

75
00:05:04,140 --> 00:05:06,780
it does not replace the separately retained
world coordinates.

76
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:11,610
Track the tall tower’s roof corner and lower
terrace

77
00:05:11,790 --> 00:05:14,910
as orbiting changes which faces are visible.

78
00:05:15,330 --> 00:05:18,890
Screen movement alone cannot tell whether the

79
00:05:18,890 --> 00:05:21,870
point moved in the world or the camera frame
changed.

80
00:05:22,230 --> 00:05:25,850
New blocks also change the structure;

81
00:05:25,990 --> 00:05:30,590
persistent windows or railings help compare
the surrounding moments.

82
00:05:30,710 --> 00:05:35,410
Wide and close framing change a corner’s image
extent;

83
00:05:35,410 --> 00:05:39,590
calculations should retain view coordinates
and projection parameters separately.

84
00:05:39,737 --> 00:05:44,190
Multiplying a3D value by a matrix does not
immediately produce the final pixel;

85
00:05:44,470 --> 00:05:47,950
clipping,division and viewport mapping remain.

86
00:05:48,150 --> 00:05:53,070
A tower near the top of the image cannot use
worldy directly as screeny;

87
00:05:53,430 --> 00:05:55,990
camera up and screen down also differ.

88
00:05:56,230 --> 00:05:58,790
We observe visible changes in the game and
continue

89
00:05:59,190 --> 00:06:01,990
the numeric proof in our separately defined
example.

90
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:06,740
Use the previous lesson’s horizontal90-degree
view:

91
00:06:07,120 --> 00:06:13,200
zoomX1 and zoomY16/9 for a square-pixel16:9
viewport.

92
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:16,740
With near1 andfar11, map normalized

93
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:22,800
depth to0–1 using clipz equalAz plusB.

94
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:28,340
Requiring depth0 atz1 and1

95
00:06:28,340 --> 00:06:33,220
atz11 givesA1.1 andB minus1.1.

96
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:40,220
For viewz4, clipz is3.3 andw stores the
originalz4.

97
00:06:40,420 --> 00:06:44,020
The complete clip coordinate is(2,16/9,3.3,4),

98
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:48,960
from the two zoom terms and the depth mapping.

99
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:55,080
The third row contains1.1 and1;

100
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:58,520
the last contains minus1.1 and0. The final
column copiesz intow.

101
00:06:59,090 --> 00:07:03,770
Before division, clipx andy lie between minusw
andw,

102
00:07:03,990 --> 00:07:07,190
while depth lies between0 andw.

103
00:07:07,390 --> 00:07:14,070
Our point hasw4; x2 andy16/9 both lie between
minus4 and4.

104
00:07:14,330 --> 00:07:19,850
Depth3.3 is between0 and4, so the point
satisfies all three pairs of bounds.

105
00:07:20,090 --> 00:07:25,270
A point with clip x five and w four crosses
the right boundary and lies outside the view.

106
00:07:25,510 --> 00:07:28,890
We can test that boundary before calculating
its final pixel position.

107
00:07:29,090 --> 00:07:32,450
Clipping limits the viewing region; occlusion
behind another object

108
00:07:32,710 --> 00:07:36,910
is determined through a separate process such
as depth testing.

109
00:07:37,170 --> 00:07:43,010
Dividing first can lose crucial sign and
singularity handling;

110
00:07:43,290 --> 00:07:46,330
triangles must be clipped in homogeneous clip
space before division.

111
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:51,660
Carrying the cube changes its location in the
chamber;

112
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:54,240
compare it with the fixed pillars.

113
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:59,400
The player sets down the cube from another
view and connects the laser;

114
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:03,120
world placement and image position are
different information.

115
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:08,540
Compare the large close cube with wider
chamber views;

116
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:11,800
image proportions alone do not reveal its
exact viewz.

117
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:17,660
The portal view is another viewing problem;

118
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:20,980
we use visible cube and pillar landmarks
without inferring portal rendering internals.

119
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:24,560
Laser activation changes the route and exit
indicators;

120
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:28,820
distinguish game-state changes from
camera-coordinate changes.

121
00:08:29,180 --> 00:08:31,940
Real gameplay mixes changes; a one-point
calculation

122
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:36,640
isolates world placement,camera and projection
one at a time.

123
00:08:37,110 --> 00:08:41,450
Consider a separate triangle with its near
plane at one.

124
00:08:41,650 --> 00:08:46,550
Vertex A has view depth zero point five zero,
while the other two vertices have depth two.

125
00:08:46,810 --> 00:08:49,550
Discarding the whole triangle would remove its
visible portion;

126
00:08:49,550 --> 00:08:55,390
compute the two edge intersections instead.

127
00:08:55,750 --> 00:09:00,690
Travel along an edge from depth two toward
zero point five zero. The near-plane

128
00:09:00,850 --> 00:09:03,570
intersection at depth one occurs two-thirds of
the way along that directed edge.

129
00:09:04,050 --> 00:09:06,410
Interpolate the other coordinates at the same
parameter;

130
00:09:06,410 --> 00:09:10,910
our intersections havex minusone-third and
plusone-third.

131
00:09:11,170 --> 00:09:14,950
The two inside vertices and two intersections
form a quadrilateral,

132
00:09:15,490 --> 00:09:18,490
which can be triangulated for drawing.

133
00:09:18,810 --> 00:09:21,610
At each new intersection, clipz0 andw1 match
the near boundary;

134
00:09:21,990 --> 00:09:25,190
divide only after completing clipping.

135
00:09:25,470 --> 00:09:29,990
Correct the source’s wording: a small
positivez makesw small,

136
00:09:30,350 --> 00:09:34,970
while1/w and divided results can grow large.

137
00:09:34,970 --> 00:09:41,030
A near plane limits that unstable region, but
an overly large value cuts

138
00:09:41,430 --> 00:09:44,530
nearby walls and floors; choose it with range
and precision in mind.

139
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,640
Normalized depth may instead use minus1 to1,

140
00:09:48,820 --> 00:09:52,540
requiring clipz between minusw andw.

141
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:58,840
Keep near one and far eleven. The multiplier
for view z is one point two zero.

142
00:09:59,100 --> 00:10:02,580
Subtract two point two zero from that result,
then divide by w.

143
00:10:02,740 --> 00:10:05,560
Atz4 this gives clipz2.6 and depth0.65,

144
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:10,920
rather than0.825 from the previous convention.

145
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:17,080
Adding one to zero point six five gives one
point six five. Dividing by two gives

146
00:10:17,460 --> 00:10:20,740
zero point eight two five. The two depth
conventions describe the same position.

147
00:10:21,200 --> 00:10:25,340
For right-handed negativez-forward camera
coordinates and column vectors,

148
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:28,380
transpose and account for the changed inputz
sign.

149
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:35,340
A column matrix with last row(0,0,−1,0) makesw
equal−z;

150
00:10:35,760 --> 00:10:39,280
our depth4 is written as viewz−4 in that
camera convention.

151
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:47,180
Orthographic depth keeps w at one and changes
linearly. For zero to one, subtract one

152
00:10:47,180 --> 00:10:54,160
from view z and divide by ten. For minus one
to one, double that result and subtract one.

153
00:10:54,560 --> 00:10:57,220
Compare the four conventions rather than just
formula names;

154
00:10:57,480 --> 00:11:03,340
historical hardware restrictions do not define
all modern graphics systems.

155
00:11:03,810 --> 00:11:07,810
New construction changes the front terrace and
long roof;

156
00:11:08,150 --> 00:11:11,030
watch the interior opening and rear tower
together.

157
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:14,610
Orbiting hides part of the tower behind a
front roof;

158
00:11:14,930 --> 00:11:17,930
that differs from leaving the screen boundary.

159
00:11:18,170 --> 00:11:22,030
An elevated view reveals previously hidden
platforms;

160
00:11:22,350 --> 00:11:25,770
treating all invisibility as one problem makes
debugging difficult.

161
00:11:26,110 --> 00:11:28,350
First ask whether a point is within the view,

162
00:11:28,750 --> 00:11:31,790
then consider its relation to foreground
objects.

163
00:11:31,790 --> 00:11:36,850
We have not identified a near-plane hole here;

164
00:11:37,350 --> 00:11:40,510
the observed changes are construction and
viewpoint-dependent occlusion.

165
00:11:40,910 --> 00:11:45,590
The image does not establish the game’s near
distance or depth format;

166
00:11:46,010 --> 00:11:49,090
our defined triangle supplies the numeric
clipping proof.

167
00:11:49,330 --> 00:11:53,130
Keep the same landmark through closer or wider
views;

168
00:11:53,530 --> 00:11:56,390
switching to another roof changes the object
of comparison.

169
00:11:56,770 --> 00:12:00,050
Next we map surviving points to screen
coordinates,

170
00:12:00,050 --> 00:12:03,790
still distinguishing camera axes from pixel
axes.

171
00:12:04,260 --> 00:12:09,100
Return to our point in clip space: horizontal
two, vertical sixteen ninths,

172
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:15,380
depth three point three, and fourth component
four. Divide all four components by four.

173
00:12:15,420 --> 00:12:24,260
NDC is(0.5,4/9,0.825); these are not yet pixel
coordinates.

174
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:28,840
Use a viewport starting at(100,50),

175
00:12:28,840 --> 00:12:34,240
width800 and height450: a rectangle offset
within the full image.

176
00:12:34,260 --> 00:12:37,740
For horizontal position, add one to the
normalized value,

177
00:12:38,020 --> 00:12:42,680
divide by two, multiply by the width, and add
the window origin.

178
00:12:43,020 --> 00:12:45,820
Zero point five maps to three-quarters of the
window width.

179
00:12:46,060 --> 00:12:51,620
The result isx700, two hundred pixels right of
the viewport’s center500.

180
00:12:51,900 --> 00:12:57,980
For vertical position, subtract four ninths
from one to reverse the upward-positive

181
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:01,720
direction. Multiply by half of height four
hundred fifty, then add fifty.

182
00:13:01,980 --> 00:13:05,600
The vertical position is one hundred
seventy-five. The same point therefore has

183
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:08,800
horizontal position seven hundred and vertical
position one hundred seventy-five.

184
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:16,820
This is a continuous screen position;
pixel-center and coverage rules

185
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:20,740
belong to rasterization. Do not blindly round
the vertex to an integer.

186
00:13:21,230 --> 00:13:24,010
We expressed the same point in local, world,
and camera frames,

187
00:13:24,330 --> 00:13:26,410
then used the projection matrix to produce
clip coordinates.

188
00:13:26,810 --> 00:13:30,250
After clipping, division by w, and viewport
mapping,

189
00:13:30,690 --> 00:13:33,630
our point appears at horizontal seven hundred
and vertical one hundred seventy-five.

190
00:13:34,450 --> 00:13:37,450
Next, examine how stored depth differs from
distance

191
00:13:37,750 --> 00:13:40,750
and why texture interpolation still needs w.
